  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1233 AM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nIn the past 24 hrs, models have made a few changes in the expected \nsolution that will have dramatic effects for southern CO. First, the \nupper trough is located slightly more to the west, outside of the \n\"sweet\" spot for copious precip amounts for much of the forecast \narea save for the Continental Divide. Second, the remnants of \nTropical Storm Simon is expected to make landfall along the Mexico \ncoastline much farther south, and will be less of a player in \nadditional available moisture.\n\nSunday and Monday...Strong Pacific low pressure trough digs south \nalong the West Coast but really remains anchored there on Sunday and \nfor much of Monday. This will lead to enhanced shower and storm \nchances for the higher terrain, with a focus on western CO and the \nContinental Divide. Meanwhile, the plains will remain very warm and \nat times breezy. Latest QPF amounts are showing around .2 to .3 of \nan inch total over both days for the mts, and around an inch for the \neastern San Juans with much of that coming Sunday. Given the \ndecreasing QPF amounts, do not feel a Flash Flood Watch will be \nneeded. As for temps, the plains will remain hot and above normal \nwith highs in the 80s to around 90F. For the high valleys, temps \nwill climb into the 60s to around 70F.\n\nTuesday...Models finally start the upper low moving across the Great \nBasin on Tue, reaching the northwestern quad of CO by the evening. \nThis path will lead to continued precip chances for the higher \nterrain that will likely spread to the Pikes Peak region through the \nevening, as well as brisk winds for the San Luis Valley and portions \nof the eastern plains through the day along with perhaps some \nisolated showers. The cold front originally slated for Tue morning \nstill is there, though it backdoors into the eastern plains and does \nnot look as strong as previously expected. Regardless, cooler temps \nare forecast across the board for Tue with highs in the 60s for the \nhigh valleys, and 70s to around 80F for the plains. This is also \nwhen temps cool enough for precip to finally switch over to snow for \nthe mts, but weakening moisture plume has really put a damper on \npotential new snow amounts. Latest forecast amounts are coming in \naround 2 to maybe 3 inches for the peaks of the eastern San Juan Mts \nand for the peaks in Lake County.\n\nWednesday through Friday...Upper low system crosses over CO and WY \nquickly on Wed, reaching the Dakotas by Thu morning, allowing strong \nnorth to northwest flow to sweep across CO both Thu and Fri. \nPrecipitation chances are fairly dismal across the forecast area \nafter Wed evening, but temperatures certainly begin a cooling trend \nthrough the end of the week. In fact, freezing overnight \ntemperatures are forecast for the high valleys starting Wednesday \nnight, and continuing each night into the weekend. Moore",
